course: world
lesson: 129
title: Being on time around the world, waiting your turn and deadlines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Being "on time" means different things in different places. Japan's trains are famous for it: in 2017 a company said sorry in public because a train left 20 seconds early, since early is wrong too when people plan to catch it. In Germany on time often means a few minutes early; in Brazil, arriving a bit late to a party at a friend's home is normal and polite, though school and work start on the dot in both.
explain: Daily rhythms differ too: in Spain dinner is often at half nine at night, while many English families eat at half five or six. Neither is wrong; it's worth learning how a place uses the clock before judging it.
explain: Time is shared. Waiting your turn in a queue is fair because everyone's time counts the same, and pushing in steals other people's minutes. A bus keeps a timetable for everyone further down the road, so it doesn't wait, however loudly you shout.
explain: Deadlines go better little and often: 20 minutes a day for 2 weeks is 280 minutes, over 4 and a half hours, with no panic, while starting a 3-hour job at 8 pm on Sunday ends at 11 pm, 2 hours past bedtime. Putting things off is called procrastination, and everyone does it sometimes.
explain: Being on time shows respect for other people's time, and lateness is mostly fixed by habits: pack your bag the night before, leave a bit earlier, and arrive a few minutes early, like Ella's mum, who sits in the car at 9:50 for a 10:00 meeting.
fact: In 2017 a Japanese train company said sorry in public because a train left 20 seconds early.
fact: Leaving early can make people miss a train, so it counts as a mistake too.
fact: In Spain dinner is often around 21:00 to 22:00, while many English families eat at 17:30 to 18:30.
fact: In British English "half nine" means 9:30.
fact: In Germany being on time often means a little early; in Brazil arriving a bit late to a visit at a friend's home is normal.
fact: Waiting your turn in a queue is fair because everyone's time counts the same.
fact: Buses keep to a timetable and don't wait, so that the people further along the route are not made late.
fact: Putting work off to the last minute is called procrastination; a deadline is when the work must be handed in.
fact: 20 minutes a day for 14 days is 280 minutes, which is 4 hours 40 minutes.
fact: A 3-hour job started at 8 pm ends at 11 pm.
fact: If 30 children each kept a teacher waiting 5 minutes, she would lose 150 minutes.
fact: Being 5 minutes late on each of 5 school days adds up to 25 minutes a week.
fact: "At 10 sharp" and "on the dot" mean exactly on time; "grand" in Ireland means fine.
exercise: Why did a Japanese train company say sorry in 2017? | A train left 20 seconds early | A train was 2 hours late | A train went to the wrong city | A train ran out of seats
exercise: Why can leaving early be a problem for a train? | People who planned to catch it may miss it | It wastes a lot of electricity on the track | The driver gets into trouble with the police for speeding | Early trains are always far too crowded to use
exercise: What does "half nine" mean in Britain? | 9:30 | 8:30 | 9:15 | 4:30
exercise: Around what time is dinner often eaten in Spain? | 21:00 to 22:00 | 17:00 to 18:00 | 12:00 to 13:00 | 07:00 to 08:00
exercise: Lukas arrives at 2:55 for a 3:00 playdate. How early is he? | 5 minutes | 55 minutes | 3 minutes | 15 minutes
exercise: Ana arrives at 3:30 for a 3:00 playdate. How late is that by the clock? | 30 minutes | 3 minutes | 33 minutes | 1 hour 30 minutes
exercise: Why is waiting your turn in a queue fair? | Everyone's time counts the same | The fastest person should go first | Queues make the shops sell a lot more | Teachers really like nice long queues
exercise: Holly starts a 3-hour project at 8 pm. When will she finish? | 11 pm | 10 pm | 9 pm | 12 midnight
exercise: Holly's bedtime is 9 pm. How long after bedtime will she finish? | 2 hours | 3 hours | 1 hour | 11 hours
exercise: What is procrastination? | Putting things off until later | Doing work very early | Working too fast | Leaving your homework at home
exercise: Jake does 20 minutes a day for 14 days. How many minutes is that? | 280 | 34 | 140 | 2,800
exercise: Why doesn't a bus wait for someone running late? | It would make everyone further along the route late | Bus drivers are told to be mean | Buses can't stop once the doors close | Buses only wait for grown-ups
exercise: If 30 children each kept the teacher waiting 5 minutes, how much time would she lose? | 150 minutes | 35 minutes | 30 minutes | 300 minutes
exercise: What does "grand" mean when an Irish dad says it? | Fine, OK | Very big | A thousand pounds | Grandad
exercise: Ella's mum arrives at 9:50 for a meeting at "10 sharp". How early is she? | 10 minutes | 50 minutes | 1 minute | 15 minutes
exercise: Kofi is 5 minutes late every school day for a week. How many minutes is that? | 25 | 10 | 5 | 50
test: Callum: "Sorry for being early? That's mad." Why did the Japanese company say sorry? | Passengers who came on time could have missed the train | It's the law to say sorry every day | The train broke a world speed record | It arrived early at the end of the line and the driver got told off
test: Tom, from Leeds: "Dinner at half nine?! I'm in bed by then, me." Maria: "Is normal in Spain!" Who's wrong? | Neither: countries have different daily rhythms | Maria: dinner must always be eaten at six o'clock | Tom: everyone in the world should eat at half nine | Maria: Spain has no evening, so dinner is at night
test: Lukas comes at 2:55 and Ana at 3:30 for a 3:00 playdate, and both think they were on time. What's the best way to see it? | Different places mean different things by "on time"; ask what's expected | Lukas was rude to arrive early | Ana was on time by the clock | Both were late, as playdates have no time
test: Kai: "Oi, back of the line, mate!" Ollie: "Chill, it's only one person." There are 10 kids ahead, each taking 1 minute. What did Ollie do? | He skipped 10 minutes of waiting and made everyone else wait longer | He saved everybody some time | He made no difference at all | He made the queue move faster
test: Holly: "Project's due Monday, I'll smash it Sunday night from 8." It takes 3 hours, and bedtime's 9. What happens? | She finishes at 11 pm, 2 hours past bedtime | She finishes at 9 pm, right on her bedtime, no problem | She finishes at 10 pm, just 1 hour after bedtime | She finishes by 8 pm, before she has even started
test: Jake: "20 minutes a day? That's nothing, innit." Over 2 weeks, how much is it? | 280 minutes, over 4 and a half hours | 40 minutes | 2 hours exactly | 20 hours
test: Amir, running at the bus: "Wait, ya muppet!" The bus goes. Whose job was it to be there on time? | Amir's: the bus keeps a timetable for everyone | The driver's: he must wait for every shout | Nobody's: buses come whenever they like | The other passengers': they should hold the door
test: Leo's dad, from Dublin, hears the food will be 20 minutes: "Grand, we'll have a drink so." ("Grand" means fine.) What's sensible about this? | He fills the waiting time with something nice | He shouts at the waiter to hurry up | He leaves without eating | He complains to everyone that 20 minutes is basically forever
test: Mia's mum: "Your teacher's time is precious too." Mia: "It's only 5 minutes, it's giving drama." ("It's giving drama" means you're making a fuss.) What's Mum's point? | Others lose time too; being on time shows respect | Being 5 minutes late never matters to anybody at all | Teachers always have plenty of extra time to spare | Only teachers are ever allowed to turn up late to class
test: Ella's mum sits in the car from 9:50 for a "10 sharp" meeting. Why? | Arriving a bit early means she's never late and feels calm | She thought the meeting was at 9:50 | She wants to walk in 10 minutes late | She's waiting for the car engine to cool down before she gets out of it
test: Kofi's teacher: "Late once is an accident. Late every day is a choice." What would fix Kofi's lateness? | Better habits, like packing his bag the night before | Better excuses every morning | Asking the whole school to start half an hour later every day | Blaming the bus every day
test: What would happen if a bus waited 2 minutes for a late runner at each of 10 stops? | It would be about 20 minutes late by the end | It would still be exactly on time | It would be just 2 minutes late in total | It would arrive early
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the early departure of a train is regarded in Japan as a failure. | Passengers arriving punctually may thereby be deprived of their journey | Early departure contravenes the speed limits of the railway | Early departure increases the consumption of electricity | Early trains are invariably overcrowded
exam: Given that a pupil devotes twenty minutes daily to a project over a period of fourteen days, determine the aggregate time so expended. | Two hundred and eighty minutes | One hundred and forty minutes | Thirty-four minutes | Two thousand eight hundred minutes
exam: A task requiring three hours is commenced at eight o'clock in the evening. Ascertain the hour of its completion. | Eleven o'clock in the evening | Ten o'clock in the evening | Nine o'clock in the evening | Midnight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the term "procrastination". | The putting off of a task until a later time | The completion of a task well before its deadline | The division of a task among several different persons | The measurement of time by means of a water clock
